# Swift/iOS security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' security rules to iOS/Apple-platform risks (OWASP Mobile Top 10) and the safe framework API for each.

## Secrets and credential storage

- Store credentials, tokens and refresh tokens in the Keychain (`Security`
  framework or a thin wrapper), never in `UserDefaults` or a plain file.
- Never hardcode an API key, client secret or signing credential as a string
  literal. Load it from build-time configuration (an `.xcconfig` value injected
  into `Info.plist`, or a secrets manager fetched at runtime) excluded from
  source control; a literal key in a diff is a finding, not a style nit.
- Set Keychain accessibility to `kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly` or
  stricter for anything sensitive.

## Network security (ATS and transport)

- Leave App Transport Security at its default (HTTPS-only, TLS 1.2+); no
  `NSAllowsArbitraryLoads` or blanket `NSExceptionDomains` bypass in `Info.plist`.
  Scope any exception to the exact domain and minimum relaxation, with a comment.
- Use `URLSession`'s default trust evaluation; no `URLSessionDelegate` that
  accepts any server certificate outside a short-lived local/test configuration,
  and never shipped.

## Biometric authentication

- A `LAContext` biometric result is a local presence gate that unlocks a
  locally-held secret, not proof of identity to a backend.
- Check the specific `LAError` case on failure (`.userCancel`,
  `.biometryNotAvailable`, `.biometryLockout`, ...) and present a fallback
  (passcode, credential re-entry); do not treat all failures alike or retry silently.
- Do not cache "biometric succeeded" as a long-lived boolean; re-evaluate where
  access is needed.

## Input handling and injection-adjacent risks

- Parameterize any `NSPredicate`/Core Data query built from user-influenced input
  rather than interpolating into the predicate format string.
- Validate any URL or deep link the app opens (`UIApplication.open`, a Universal
  Link handler) before acting on it.

## Logging

- Never log a token, password or full PII payload to persistent device logs
  (`print`, unredacted `os_log`/`Logger` interpolation); use
  `privacy: .private` or omit it.
